## Ossie interchange

`gnd export ossie MODEL --out FILE` projects a standalone GND model to an
Apache Ossie ontology document, version `0.3.0.dev0`, using the
`ontology/ontology.json` schema from commit
`ee89dbdc2844d8b7d293ed42c8613928bb99505f`. The optional parser oracle comes
from `apache/ossie-converters` at
`83ddc294c8840f5f497a2bdbec2348a128fc2254`. YAML is the default and uses 1.1-compatible scalar spelling. Select
`--format json` for canonical JSON. The source loader refuses include-bearing
models with `include:unsupported-route` before resolving their dependencies.

| Source | Core projection |
|---|---|
| Object types and subtypes | EntityType or ValueType concepts, with subtype parents in `extends`. Safe unique names are preserved. Other names get deterministic ID-based aliases. |
| Value primitives | The corresponding Ossie built-in, with Number projected as Decimal and Duration as Integer. The sidecar retains those source types and Binary. |
| Popular and general references | A generated binary OneToOne relationship and an `identify_by` entry. |
| External references | `identify_by` on functional binary relationships sharing the first-role entity. Unsupported reference details travel in the sidecar. |
| Fact types and readings | One relationship under its first-role concept. The forward placeholders bind by source role position. Explicit inverse qualifiers bind by role name. A bare binary inverse reverses the bindings. Additional roles have explicit names. |
| Confirmed alethic internal uniqueness | OneToOne for both directions of a binary. ManyToOne for an n−1 determinant, with the dependent role last. Reading bindings retain source order even when declaration roles move. |
| Reviewed constraints and derivations | `requires` and `derived_by` at the scope described below. |
| Other source rules, provenance, modality and operations | The source values travel in the sidecar. |

The formula projection supports confirmed alethic rules in these shapes.
Players are String value types without subtype or supertype links, with
an Integer value type in the compared role for the integer guard.
A binary relationship can require an integer second-role value to be at
most a literal, or forbid a self-link between two roles of the same type.
Unary relations over the same object type support subset, equality and
exclusion, with both equality directions published as one group. A
String value concept can declare a finite enum of representable string
literals. Ontology scope can limit the cardinality of a named object type.

Without a sidecar, import admits the supported declarations of a foreign
ontology. It uses deterministic GND IDs, direct references through a
single supported identifying link, subtype links and first readings that
bind every role of a supported relationship. Unsupported composite identifiers
are recorded as refusals. Root entities that need one of those identifiers
and relationships that refer to those entities are omitted. Formulas,
mappings and additional readings receive per-pointer refusals. The exact
pinned Flights example carries verifiable Apache Ossie provenance for its
projected multiplicities. Multiplicity declarations from unversioned
foreign inputs are reported as refusals.

The reader validates with vendored schemas and caps each input at 10 MiB.
Export applies the same limit to its three artifacts. The import command
publishes a JSON model and `MODEL.loss.json` together, or uses the report
path given by `--loss-report`. The report names its source digest and
available source model identity, and records one disposition per tracked
pointer. Exit 3 gives a value-free `ossie-import:<rule>` refusal. Exit 11
indicates an output collision.

### The import loss report

The import report uses `gnd-ossie-import-loss/1`. It is a different document
from the export report, which uses `gnd-ossie-loss/1`, and it is not checked
against the packaged export report schema. Its members are:

| Member | Value |
|---|---|
| `schema` | `gnd-ossie-import-loss/1` |
| `document_sha256` | The bare hexadecimal SHA-256 of the exact ontology bytes that were read. |
| `source_model_identity` | The sidecar's `model_identity`, or `null` for an ontology without a sidecar. |
| `source_identity_verified` | `true` when the restored model's identity equals `source_model_identity`, otherwise `false`. It is `false` without a sidecar. |
| `dispositions` | One row per tracked pointer, each with `pointer`, `disposition` and `reason`. |

With a sidecar and an adjacent export report, the rows are the export
report's rows, unchanged, and their pointers address the GND source. With a
sidecar and no report, every restored source pointer has the disposition
`extension` and the reason `gnd-source`. Without a sidecar, the pointers
address the ontology document, and each row is one of these:

| Disposition | Reason | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `native` | `projection` | The import read this node into the model. |
| `refused` | `foreign:unsupported` | The import omitted this node. |
| `refused` | `foreign:decimal-domain` | A value concept extends the built-in Decimal. The link is omitted. |

def canonical_json_bytes(value: object, *, exact_numbers: bool = False) -> bytes:
    """Return the one canonical JSON document form every stored digest is over.

    ``exact_numbers`` opts bounded proposal values into lossless Decimal output;
    the default path and its historical bytes are unchanged.

    Sorted keys, ``(",", ":")`` separators, literal non-ASCII, no non-finite
    numbers, and exactly one trailing newline.  A pydantic model is dumped in
    JSON mode first.  Every recorded ``*_sha256`` in this repository is a digest
    of these bytes, so the shape is pinned by assertion in ``tests/test_artifacts.py``
    rather than left to inspection.
    """

    document = value.model_dump(mode="json") if isinstance(value, BaseModel) else value
    if exact_numbers:

        def encode(item):
            if isinstance(item, Decimal):
                if not item.is_finite():
                    raise ValueError("nonfinite exact JSON number")
                return str(item)
            if isinstance(item, dict):
                if any(not isinstance(key, str) for key in item):
                    raise ValueError("exact JSON objects require string keys")
                return "{" + ",".join(encode(k) + ":" + encode(item[k]) for k in sorted(item)) + "}"
            if isinstance(item, list):
                return "[" + ",".join(map(encode, item)) + "]"
            return json.dumps(item, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False, separators=(",", ":"))

        return (encode(document) + "\n").encode()
    return (
        json.dumps(
            document,
            ensure_ascii=False,
            sort_keys=True,
            separators=(",", ":"),
            allow_nan=False,
        )
        + "\n"
    ).encode("utf-8")


